fold-mem-offsets: a use that is a memory root must use the folded register only as that root's base (m68k memory-to-memory moves).

Author: Bernie Innocenti (the base-register check); gcc14-cross-amix
  contributors (the destination-address check, 2026-11-20)
Origin: https://github.com/AmigaPorts/gcc (branch amiga16.2, tag
  m68k-amigaos-gcc-v16.2-rc24) commit 1e6714c8f3756fc92ec93d4a3a25a49333b5407b,
  the same change as toolchain/patches-gcc16/0009-fold-mem-offsets-mem2mem.patch
  on the GCC 16 build, extended here.
Upstream: not in GCC.  No fold-mem-offsets fix on releases/gcc-14, -15 or -16
  covers it (checked 2026-11-20); master has rewritten the pass on RTL-SSA.
Licence: GPL-3.0-or-later, as the GCC sources it changes.
Base: GCC 14.2.0; gcc-0001 and gcc-0002 do not touch this file.

The fault: fold_offsets accepts every use of a foldable add that is in
can_fold_insns, and every memory root is.  A memory-to-memory move is a
root for its SOURCE operand only (get_fold_mem_root takes SET_SRC first),
but the folded register may be the index of its DESTINATION:

  d0 = d0 + -4                          deleted ("Instruction folded")
  mem (a0 + d0) = mem (a1 + 64)         keeps d0, now 4 too high
  a2 = a2 + d0
  mem (a2) = ...                        offset 0 -> -4

so `a[n - 1] = s->v; b[n - 1] = f ();` stores a[n].  Measured with GCC
14.2.0 -O2 -m68020-60: move.l 64(%a1),(%a0,%d0.l), d0 = n * 4.

AmigaPorts' check refuses a root use whose base register is not the folded
one.  The second check refuses a root use where the folded register is the
source's base and also appears in a memory destination's address, which
would keep its old offset for the same reason.  Both only refuse folds.

The toolchain's specs also pass -fno-fold-mem-offsets by default; this
patch is for code built with -ffold-mem-offsets.

diff -u a/gcc/fold-mem-offsets.cc b/gcc/fold-mem-offsets.cc
--- a/gcc/fold-mem-offsets.cc
+++ b/gcc/fold-mem-offsets.cc
@@ -254,6 +254,8 @@
 
 static HOST_WIDE_INT
 fold_offsets (rtx_insn *insn, rtx reg, bool analyze, bitmap foldable_insns);
+bool get_fold_mem_root (rtx_insn *insn, rtx *mem_out, rtx *reg_out,
+			HOST_WIDE_INT *offset_out);
 
 /*  Helper function for fold_offsets.
 
@@ -544,6 +546,21 @@
 	  if (use_set && MEM_P (SET_DEST (use_set))
 	      && reg_mentioned_p (dest, SET_SRC (use_set)))
 	    return 0;
+
+	  /* The folded offset goes into the one memory operand that
+	     get_fold_mem_root finds, so in a use that is a memory root DEST
+	     must be that operand's base register and appear nowhere else.
+	     A memory-to-memory move, which some targets have, is a root
+	     for its source operand only: DEST in its destination address
+	     would keep its old offset while the insn that set it is
+	     deleted.  */
+	  rtx use_mem, use_base;
+	  if (get_fold_mem_root (use, &use_mem, &use_base, NULL)
+	      && (REGNO (use_base) != dest_regno
+		  || (use_mem != SET_DEST (use_set)
+		      && MEM_P (SET_DEST (use_set))
+		      && reg_mentioned_p (dest, XEXP (SET_DEST (use_set), 0)))))
+	    return 0;
 	}
 
       bitmap_set_bit (&can_fold_insns, INSN_UID (def));
